>1) I have been looking for a version of NetHack 3.1.3 (for a mac) in the 
>stores, but have not been able to find one...so could anyone direct me to a 
>store or a mail-order directory in order to purchase the game?

NH isn't available in stores.  It's a public domain game--that is,
you're legally allowed to copy it.  You can get it by anonymous ftp
from linc.cis.upenn.edu (ftp linc.cis.upenn.edu;  login anonymous;
use your full username + adress as password).  I don't remember what
directory it's in--/usr/pub/games or something like that.  I believe
they have versions for lots of computers, including the Mac.  Don't do
it during work hours in PA...U.Penn gets mad.

>2) Is there any way (or version) of nethack that will allow players to get 
>together and compete over the net...

Not to my knowledge.

1)  If you mean a multiplayer version of NH, it was discussed a lot a
few months ago.  Getting the logistics right would be tough (for
example, there'd have to be a time limit on moves, and there'd have to
be something preventing players from getting to level 12 or so and
then hanging out on level 1 of the dungeon, waiting for new kids to
come in so they could beat them up and take their stuff).

2)  If you mean a way that scorefiles and bones levels could be shared
over the net, it's a nice idea.  There're still some problems that
would have to be worked out:  For example, people would have to really
promise not to "cheat":  Save file copying would be strictly
forbidden, and we'd have to come to a consensus as to whether things
like wishing for other classes' quest artifacts was kosher.  When
people do these things in their own games, I'm inclined to think that
what they do with their copy of NH is their own business, but I
wouldn't want to see a hugely communal scorefile flooded with "abused"
games.  Also, the scorefile should probably be expanded to 1000 slots
instead of 100--it's a bit of an accomplishment, at least for me, to
get on the high score list on the computer shared by the philosophy
and linguistics departments here...there are enough true nethack
wizards out there to fill a short list with ascensions filled with
dilithium crystals very quickly, shutting the rest of us completely
out.

If people just want to share bones files, there's a way to do it that
might require less mucking about in the code--I'd guess (although I
haven't checked) that bones files are stored in compiled format, but
this might be changable to one readable by any machine.  Then an ftp
site could be set up to which people could upload their old bones
files--particulary unusual, exciting, or killer levels (these last
ones should probably be separated into a "Not for the faint of heart"
directory).  Anyone who wants to meet the ghost of a fellow hacker
from far away could download any or all of the files.
